    "base_task_id": "MATH_121",
    "behaviour": "shared",
    "chain": "Okay, so I need to prove that the logical statement ((P → Q) ∧ (Q → R) ∧ ¬R) → ¬P is a tautology. Hmm, a tautology means that it's always true, regardless of the truth values of P, Q, and R. I remember that tautologies can often be proven using natural deduction or by showing that the statement is logically equivalent to something that's obviously true.\n\nFirst, let me recall what each part of the statement means. P → Q is \"if P then Q,\" Q → R is \"if Q then R,\" and ¬R is \"not R.\" So the entire antecedent is \"if P then Q, and if Q then R, and not R.\" The conclusion is \"not P.\" So, if all those conditions are met, then P must be false.\n\nI think I can approach this using natural deduction. Maybe start by assuming the antecedent is true and then derive ¬P. Let me try that.\n\n1. Assume (P → Q) ∧ (Q → R) ∧ ¬R. Let's break this down into three separate assumptions: P → Q, Q → R, and ¬R.\n\n2. From ¬R, I can use the Law of Excluded Middle to consider R and ¬R. Wait, but since we have ¬R, maybe I can use that directly.\n\n3. Now, since we have Q → R and ¬R, can I derive ¬Q? Because if Q were true, then R would have to be true, which contradicts ¬R. So, by modus tollens, from Q → R and ¬R, we can conclude ¬Q.\n\n4. Now, we have ¬Q. If we have ¬Q and P → Q, can we derive ¬P? Yes, because if P were true, then Q would have to be true, which contradicts ¬Q. So, by modus tollens again, from P → Q and ¬Q, we can conclude ¬P.\n\n5. So, from the antecedent, we've derived ¬P. Therefore, the entire implication ((P → Q) ∧ (Q → R) ∧ ¬R) → ¬P is a tautology.\n\nWait, let me make sure I didn't skip any steps. Starting from the antecedent, I derived ¬P, so the implication is always true. That seems right.\n\nNow, for the real-world scenario. Let me think of a situation where P, Q, and R represent meaningful propositions. Maybe something related to health or safety.\n\nLet me define:\n- P: I have a cold.\n- Q: I have a fever.\n- R: I have a headache.\n\nSo, the statement becomes: If I have a cold, then I have a fever, and if I have a fever, then I have a headache, and I do not have a headache. Therefore, I do not have a cold.\n\nWait, that doesn't make sense because if I have a cold, I usually have a fever, and if I have a fever, I might have a headache, but the statement says I don't have a headache. So, does that mean I don't have a cold? Hmm, maybe not. Let me think of another scenario.\n\nAlternatively, maybe P: I study, Q: I pass the exam, R: I get a good grade. So, if I study, I pass the exam, and if I pass the exam, I get a good grade. But the statement says I don't get a good grade. Therefore, I don't study. That seems a bit contrived, but it's a way to apply the logical structure.\n\nWait, but in reality, getting a good grade doesn't necessarily mean you studied, but it's a common assumption. Maybe I need a better scenario.\n\nLet me think of a medical scenario. Let P be \"I have a heart condition,\" Q be \"I have a heart attack,\" and R be \"I die.\" So, the statement is: If I have a heart condition, then I have a heart attack, and if I have a heart attack, then I die, and I do not die. Therefore, I do not have a heart condition.\n\nThat makes more sense. So, if I don't die, I don't have a heart condition. That's a real-world application of the logical structure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe something in computer science. Let P be \"a system crashes,\" Q be \"it has a memory error,\" R be \"it crashes.\" So, if the system crashes, it has a memory error, and if it has a memory error, it crashes. But the statement says it doesn't crash. Therefore, it doesn't have a memory error. That's a bit of a stretch, but it's a way to model the logical implication.\n\nWait, but in reality, a system can crash without a memory error, so maybe that's not a perfect analogy. Maybe better to stick with the medical scenario.\n\nSo, in summary, the logical structure is that if you have a heart condition (P), you have a heart attack (Q), and if you have a heart attack (Q), you die (R). But since you don't die (¬R), you don't have a heart condition (¬P). This is an example of modus tollens reasoning, where if P → Q and Q → R, and ¬R, then ¬P.\n\nI think that's a solid explanation. Now, to present it formally.\n</think>\n\nThe logical statement ((P → Q) ∧ (Q → R) ∧ ¬R) → ¬P is a tautology because, through natural deduction, assuming the antecedent leads us to ¬P. Here's the step-by-step proof:\n\n1. **Assume** (P → Q) ∧ (Q → R) ∧ ¬R.\n2. **Derive** ¬R from the assumption.\n3. **From** Q → R and ¬R, **apply** modus tollens to get ¬Q.\n4. **From** P → Q and ¬Q, **apply** modus tollens to get ¬P.\n5. **Therefore**, from the assumption, we derive ¬P, proving the implication is a tautology.\n\n**Real-world scenario:**\n\nLet P be \"I have a heart condition,\" Q be \"I have a heart attack,\" and R be \"I die.\" The statement ((P → Q) ∧ (Q → R) ∧ ¬R) → ¬P means that if I have a heart condition, I will have a heart attack, and if I have a heart attack, I will die. Since I do not die (¬R), it follows that I do not have a heart condition (¬P). This illustrates modus tollens reasoning, where the conclusion ¬P is drawn from the premises P → Q, Q → R, and ¬R.\n\n**Final Answer:**\n\nThe statement is a tautology, and the real-world scenario demonstrates the application of modus tollens reasoning.\n\n\\boxed{((P \\rightarrow Q) \\land (Q \\rightarrow R) \\land \\neg R) \\rightarrow \\neg P \\text{ is a tautology.}}",
