d z=2) + (y=2 and z=2) - 2*(number of x=2, y=2, z=2)\n\nBecause (2,2,2) is counted three times in the first three terms, but it should only be counted once. So, duplicates = 4+4+4 - 2*1=10.\n\nSo, total unique blocked rooms:\n\n16 (x=2) +16 (y=2) +16 (z=2) - duplicates (10) - (2,2,2) already counted in the duplicates.\n\nWait, no, actually, the duplicates are the overlaps beyond the single (2,2,2). So, the total unique blocked rooms are:\n\n16 (x=2) +16 (y=2) +16 (z=2) - 10 (duplicates) - 1 (since (2,2,2) was subtracted three times but should only be subtracted once). Hmm, this is getting confusing.\n\nAlternatively, maybe it's easier to list all the unique rooms.\n\nBut that might take too long. Maybe I can think of the blocked rooms as:\n\nAll rooms where at least one coordinate is 2, except for (2,2,2). Because (2,2,2) is already blocked due to the sum=6.\n\nSo, total rooms with any coordinate=2: 37, as calculated earlier. But (2,2,2) is already in that count, so the unique blocked rooms are 37 -1=36.\n\nWait, no, because in the sum=6, (2,2,2) is included, so when we subtract the overlap, we have 37 -1=36.\n\nBut wait, in the inclusion-exclusion, we had 43 blocked rooms, but that included (2,2,2) twice. So, 43 -1=42. Hmm, I'm getting confused.\n\nMaybe I should just accept that there are 37 rooms with any coordinate=2, and 1 room with sum=6, which is already included in the 37. So, total blocked rooms are 37.\n\nBut wait, no, because (2,2,2) is in both the sum=6 and the any coordinate=2. So, when we count 37, we have 1 room that's double-counted. So, total unique blocked rooms are 37 -1=36.\n\nBut let me verify:\n\nTotal rooms with any coordinate=2: 37\n\nRooms where sum=6: 1\n\nSo, the intersection is 1 room, so unique blocked rooms are 37 -1=36.\n\nSo, 36 rooms are blocked, leaving 64 -36=28 rooms free.\n\nWait, but earlier I thought 21 rooms were free, but that was incorrect. It's actually 28 rooms free.\n\nWait, 4x4x4 is 64. 36 are blocked, so 64-36=28.\n\nSo