right, so the water is filling up to height h from the bottom. So, the area of the segment is equal to the area of the semicircle when lying on its side, which is (9/2)π.\n\nWait, but when lying on its side, the water is a semicircle, which is area (1/2)πr² = (9/2)π. So, when upright, the area of the segment is also (9/2)π.\n\nSo, we can set up the equation:\n\nr² arccos((r - h)/r) - (r - h) sqrt(2rh - h²) = (1/2)πr²\n\nGiven that r = 3 meters, so plugging in:\n\n3² arccos((3 - h)/3) - (3 - h) sqrt(2*3*h - h²) = (1/2)π*(3)²\n\nSimplify:\n\n9 arccos((3 - h)/3) - (3 - h) sqrt(6h - h²) = (9/2)π\n\nHmm, this seems a bit complicated. Maybe I can simplify it.\n\nLet me denote θ = arccos((3 - h)/3). Then, cosθ = (3 - h)/3, so 3 cosθ = 3 - h, so h = 3 - 3 cosθ.\n\nBut I'm not sure if that helps. Alternatively, maybe I can make a substitution.\n\nLet me let x = h, so the equation becomes:\n\n9 arccos((3 - x)/3) - (3 - x) sqrt(6x - x²) = (9/2)π\n\nThis seems difficult to solve algebraically. Maybe I can try plugging in some values for h.\n\nWait, when h = 3 meters, the area of the segment is the area of the semicircle, which is (1/2)π*(3)^2 = (9/2)π, which is exactly the area we have. So, when h = 3, the area is (9/2)π, which matches the volume when lying on its side.\n\nBut when the tank is upright, the water is filling up to h meters, so when h = 3, the tank is full, which makes sense.\n\nBut in our case, the volume is the same, so when the tank is upright, the water is also filling up to h = 3 meters, so the height is 3 meters.\n\nWait, but that can't be, because when the tank is lying on its side, the water is at 3 meters, but when it's upright, the water is also at 3 meters, so the height is the same.\n\nBut that seems counterintuitive. Wait, no, actually, when the tank is lying on its side, the water is at 3 meters, which is half the diameter, so the height is 3 meters. When it's upright, the water is also at 3 meters, so the height is 3 meters.\n\nWait, but that seems 