    "chain_full": "Okay, so I have this problem with three spheres in a 3D coordinate system. Sphere A is at (0,0,0) with radius 5, Sphere B is at (8,0,0) with radius 4, and Sphere C is at (4,6,0) with radius 3. A laser beam starts at (0,0,10) and needs to pass through all three spheres before reaching the xy-plane (which is z=0). I need to figure out if such a path exists and describe the constraints.\n\nFirst, let me visualize this. All three spheres are on the xy-plane (z=0), but Sphere A is at the origin, Sphere B is 8 units along the x-axis, and Sphere C is at (4,6,0). The laser starts at (0,0,10), which is above Sphere A, and needs to go down to the xy-plane, passing through all three spheres.\n\nSince the laser is a straight line, it must intersect each sphere in some way. For it to pass through all three spheres, the line must intersect Sphere A, Sphere B, and Sphere C. So, the line must pass through each sphere's interior. That means the line must enter each sphere, pass through it, and exit before reaching the xy-plane.\n\nI think the key here is to find a straight line that starts at (0,0,10), goes through Sphere A, then Sphere B, then Sphere C, and finally reaches the xy-plane. Alternatively, it could go through Sphere A, then Sphere C, then Sphere B, but I need to check both possibilities.\n\nBut before that, maybe I should consider the positions of the spheres. Sphere A is at the origin, so it's the closest to the starting point. Sphere B is 8 units away along the x-axis, and Sphere C is at (4,6,0), which is somewhere in the middle.\n\nI think the first step is to model the line as a parametric equation. Let me denote the line as:\n\nx = x0 + at\ny = y0 + bt\nz = z0 + ct\n\nBut since the starting point is (0,0,10), I can set x0=0, y0=0, z0=10. So, the parametric equations become:\n\nx = at\ny = bt\nz = 10 + ct\n\nNow, I need to find parameters a, b, c such that the line intersects all three spheres. Each intersection with a sphere will satisfy the sphe