 M and L.\n\nFrom statement 3, L is a subset of K. So, since E is a subset of L, then E is also a subset of K. Wait, that would mean that all E students study K. But statement 2 says that some M (which includes E) do not study K. So, if E is a subset of K, but statement 2 says that some M (which includes E) do not study K, that would mean that E is not entirely contained in K. Wait, that seems contradictory.\n\nWait, hold on. Let me clarify.\n\nStatement 1: E ⊂ M.\n\nStatement 4: E ⊂ L.\n\nStatement 3: L ⊂ K.\n\nSo, combining these, E ⊂ M, E ⊂ L, and L ⊂ K. Therefore, E ⊂ K. So, all E students study K.\n\nBut statement 2 says that some M (which includes E) do not study K. But if E is a subset of K, then all E students do study K. So, statement 2 is saying that some M do not study K, but if E is a subset of K, then all E do study K, which would mean that statement 2 is false. But statement 2 is given as true. So, this seems like a contradiction.\n\nWait, that can't be. So, if E is a subset of K, then statement 2, which says some M do not study K, would be false. But statement 2 is given as true. So, that suggests that our initial assumption that E is a subset of K is incorrect.\n\nWait, so maybe E is not entirely contained in K? But from statement 1 and 4, E is a subset of L, and L is a subset of K, so E is a subset of K. So, that seems like a contradiction.\n\nHmm, so perhaps my initial assumption is wrong. Maybe statement 3 is not necessarily implying that E is a subset of K? Wait, statement 3 says no philosopher who studies logic fails to study epistemology. So, L ⊂ K. So, if someone studies L, they must study K. But E is a subset of L, so E must be a subset of K. So, that seems unavoidable.\n\nBut then, statement 2 says that some M do not study K. But if E is a subset of K, then all E are K, so statement 2 is false. But statement 2 is given as true. So, this is a problem.\n\nWait, maybe I'm misinterpreting statement 2. Let me read it again: \"Some philosophers who study metaphysics do not study epistemology.\" So, it's saying that in the set M, there exists at least one person who is not in K. So, M ∩ ~K is non-empty.\n\nBut from statement 1 and 4, E is a subset of K, so E is a subset of K. Therefore, M ∩ K is at least E, but M ∩ ~K is non-empty because of statement 2. So, M is not entirely contained in K.\n\nBut from statement 3, L is a subset of K. So, if someone is in L, they are in K. But E is a subset of L, so E is a subset of K. So, E is a subset of K, but M is not entirely contained in K. So, M is a superset of E, but not entirely contained in K.\n\nSo, M is a set that includes E, but also includes some people not in K. So, M is E ∪ (M \\ E), where M \\ E is not entirely in K.\n\nSo, in this case, M is not entirely in K, but E is entirely in K.\n\nSo, going back to the conclusion: \"Some philosophers who study ethics do not study epistemology.\" So, E ∩ ~K is non-empty.\n\nBut from statement 3, L is a subset of K, and E is a subset of L, so E is a subset of K. Therefore, E ∩ ~K is empty. So, the conclusion is claiming that E ∩ ~K is non-empty, but in reality, it's empty.\n\nWait, that seems contradictory. So, if E is a subset of K, then the conclusion is false. But the conclusion is supposed to be \"necessarily true,\" \"possibly true,\" or \"necessarily false.\" So, if the conclusion is false, then it's not necessarily true.\n\nBut wait, maybe I made a mistake in interpreting the statements.\n\nWait, let me think again.\n\nStatement 1: All E study M. So, E ⊂ M.\n\nStatement 2: Some M do not study K. So, M ∩ ~K is non-empty.\n\nStatement 3: No L fails to study K. So, L ⊂ K.\n\nStatement 4: All E study L. So, E ⊂ L.\n\nSo, combining 1 and 4: E ⊂ L.\n\nCombining 3 and 4: E ⊂ L ⊂ K, so E ⊂ K.\n\nBut statement 2 says that M ∩ ~K is non-empty. So, M is not entirely in K.\n\nBut E is entirely in K, so M is E ∪ (M \\ E), where M \\ E is not entirely in K.\n\nTherefore, M is not entirely in K, but E is entirely in K.\n\nSo, 