    "chain": "Alright, so I've got this logic puzzle here with four colleagues: Diana, Erik, Fiona, and George. Each of them makes a statement about who will receive a promotion. The catch is that exactly three of these four statements are true, and exactly one is false. I need to figure out who gets the promotion based on their statements.\n\nLet me list out the statements again to make sure I have them all:\n\n1. Diana says: \"Either Erik or Fiona will get the promotion, but not both.\"\n2. Erik says: \"If I get the promotion, then George will not get it.\"\n3. Fiona says: \"Diana and I will both get promotions, or neither of us will.\"\n4. George says: \"Exactly two people will receive promotions.\"\n\nOkay, so four statements, three true, one false. I need to figure out who gets the promotion. Hmm, let's break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, let's consider each statement and what it implies.\n\nStarting with Diana's statement: \"Either Erik or Fiona will get the promotion, but not both.\" So, Diana is saying that exactly one of Erik or Fiona gets a promotion, and the other doesn't. So, Diana is asserting that exactly one of E or F is promoted.\n\nErik's statement: \"If I get the promotion, then George will not get it.\" So, in logical terms, this is like saying if E is true, then G is false. Symbolically, E → ¬G. This is an implication, so if E is true, G must be false. But if E is false, the implication is automatically true regardless of G.\n\nFiona's statement: \"Diana and I will both get promotions, or neither of us will.\" So, Fiona is saying that either both Diana and herself (which is redundant, so just Diana) and George get promotions, or neither of them gets a promotion. Wait, hold on. Let me parse that again. It says \"Diana and I will both get promotions, or neither of us will.\" Hmm, so it's a bit ambiguous. Is it Diana and George both getting promotions, or neither of Diana and George? Or is it Diana and Fiona both getting promotions, or neither of them? Wait, the wording is a bit unclear.\n\nWait, let me read it again: \"Diana and I will both get promotions, or neither of us will.\" So, \"Diana and I\" is redundant, so it's just \"I and I\" which is just Diana. So, it's saying Diana and George both get promotions, or neither of us (which is Diana and George) get promotions. So, essentially, it's saying that Diana and George both get promotions, or neither of them get promotions. So, that's a biconditional statement. So, either both Diana and George get promotions, or neither does. So, it's equivalent to (D ∧ G) ↔ (¬D ∧ ¬G). So, either both Diana and George get promotions, or neither does. So, that's a strong statement. Either both are promoted or both aren't.\n\nGeorge's statement: \"Exactly two people will receive promotions.\" So, exactly two of the four will get promotions. So, exactly two people are promoted.\n\nSo, summarizing:\n\n1. Diana: Either E or F, but not both. So, exactly one of E or F is true.\n2. Erik: If E, then ¬G.\n3. Fiona: Either (D and G) or (¬D and ¬G). So, a biconditional: (D ∧ G) ↔ (¬D ∧ ¬G).\n4. George: Exactly two people get promotions.\n\nWe know that exactly three statements are true, one is false. So, three of these four are true, one is false.\n\nLet me consider the possibilities. Since George's statement is that exactly two people get promotions, if George is telling the truth, then exactly two people are promoted. If George is lying, then the number of promotions is not two, so either zero, one, three, or four.\n\nBut let's see if George's statement can be true or false.\n\nFirst, let's assume George is telling the truth. Then, exactly two people are promoted. Let's see if that's possible.\n\nIf exactly two people are promoted, then let's see what that implies for the other statements.\n\nIf exactly two people are promoted, then let's consider who could be promoted.\n\nCase 1: Diana and George are promoted. So, D and G.\n\nBut then, let's check the other statements.\n\nDiana's statement: Either E or F, but not both. If D and G are promoted, then E and F are not. So, Diana's statement is true because exactly one of E or F is promoted.\n\nErik's statement: If E, then ¬G. But in this case, E is false because E is not promoted. So, the implication is true regardless of G. So, since E is false, the implication is automatically true. So, if E is false, the implication is true, so regardless of G, the statement is true. So, since E is false, the implication is true, so the statement is true.\n\nFiona's statement: Either (D and G) or (¬D and ¬G). Since D and G are both promoted, this is true. So, Fiona's statement is true.\n\nGeorge's statement: Exactly two people are promoted. Since exactly two are promoted, George is telling the truth.\n\nSo, in this case, all four statements would be true, but the problem states that exactly three are true and one is false. So, this case is impossible because it would result in all four statements being true, which violates the condition.\n\nCase 2: Diana and someone else is promoted. Let's see.\n\nIf Diana is promoted, then D is true. Let's see what that implies.\n\nIf D is true, then let's look at Diana's statement: Either E or F, but not both. So, exactly one of E or F is true.\n\nErik's statement: If E, then ¬G. So, if E is true, then G is false.\n\nFiona's statement: Either (D and G) or (¬D and ¬G). Since D is true, this simplifies to (D and G) or (¬D and ¬G). Since D is true, (D and G) is true if G is true, else false. (¬D and ¬G) is false because D is true. So, Fiona's statement simplifies to (D and G). So, if D is true, then Fiona's statement is equivalent to G.\n\nGeorge's statement: Exactly two people are promoted. If D is true, then G is either true or false.\n\nSo, let's consider two subcases:\n\nSubcase 2a: D and G are both promoted. So, D and G.\n\nThen, since D is true, G is true. So, from Fiona's statement, G is true, so Fiona's statement is true.\n\nFrom Diana's statement, since E or F, but not both. So, exactly one of E or F is true.\n\nFrom Erik's statement: If E, then ¬G. But G is true, so if E is true, then G must be false, which contradicts G being true. Therefore, E must be false. So, E is false.\n\nSo, E is false. So, from Diana's statement, since E is false, the statement \"Either E or F, but not both\" is true because F must be true (since exactly one of E or F is true). So, F is true.\n\nSo, in this subcase, D and G are true, E is false, F is true.\n\nSo, promotions: D and G.\n\nNow, let's check George's statement: Exactly two people are promoted. But in this case, D and G are promoted, so that's two. So, George's statement is true.\n\nSo, in this subcase, all four statements: Diana, Erik, Fiona, George. Let's check each:\n\n1. Diana: True (E or F, but not both. E is false, F is true. So, exactly one of E or F is true. True.\n2. Erik: If E, then ¬G. E is false, so implication is true. True.\n3. Fiona: Either (D and G) or (¬D and ¬G). D and G are both true, so this is true. True.\n4. George: Exactly two people are promoted. D and G are promoted, so exactly two. True.\n\nWait, but in this case, all four statements are true, which is not allowed because exactly three are true. So, this subcase is invalid.\n\nSubcase 2b: D is promoted, but G is not. So, D is true, G is false.\n\nFrom Fiona's statement: Either (D and G) or (¬D and ¬G). Since D is true and G is false, (D and G) is false, and (¬D and ¬G) is also false. So, Fiona's statement is false.\n\nBut if George's statement is false, then exactly two people are not promoted. But in this case, D is promoted, G is not. So, only D is promoted, which is one person. So, George's statement is false because it's not exactly two. So, that's okay.\n\nBut let's check the other statements.\n\nDiana's statement: Either E or F, but not both. So, exactly one of E or F is true.\n\nErik's statement: If E, then ¬G. Since G is false, ¬G is true. So, if E is true, then ¬G is true, which is consistent. If E is false, the implication is automatically true.\n\nSo, let's see. If D is true, G is false, and exactly one of E or F is true.\n\nSo, let's consider E and F.\n\nCase 2b1: E is true, F is false.\n\nSo, E is true, F is false.\n\nFrom Diana's statement: Either E or F, but not both. Since E is true and F is false, this is true.\n\nFrom Fiona's statement: Since D is true and G is false, Fiona's statement is false.\n\nFrom Erik's statement: If E is true, then ¬G. Since E is true and G is false, this is true.\n\nGeorge's statement: Exactly two people are promoted. But D is true, G is false, so only D is promoted. So, George's statement is false.\n\nSo, in this case, statements:\n\n1. Diana: True\n2. Erik: True\n3. Fiona: False\n4. George: False\n\nSo, three statements are true (Diana, Erik, George) and one is false (Fiona). That fits the condition.\n\nWait, but hold on. If E is true, F is false, D is true, G is false.\n\nSo, promotions: D and E.\n\nBut let's check Fiona's statement again. Fiona says either (D and G) or (¬D and ¬G). Since D is true and G is false, this is false. So, Fiona is lying.\n\nBut in this case, three statements are true: Diana, Erik, George. So, that works.\n\nBut wait, let's check if this is consistent.\n\nDiana: E or F, but not both. E is true, F is false. So, exactly one of E or F is true. True.\n\nErik: If E, then ¬G. E is true, G is false. So, implication is true. True.\n\nFiona: Either (D and G) or (¬D and ¬G). D is true, G is false, so (D and G) is false, (¬D and ¬G) is false. So, Fiona is lying. False.\n\nGeorge: Exactly two people are promoted. D is true, E is true, so two people. So, George is lying. False.\n\nSo, three true, one false. That works.\n\nBut wait, in this case, D and E are promoted, G is not, and F is not. So, two promotions, which is what George said, but George is lying because it's not exactly two. So, that's consistent.\n\nBut let's check another subcase.\n\nSubcase 2c: D is promoted, G is promoted. So, D and G.\n\nBut we already considered this earlier, and it resulted in all four statements being true, which is invalid.\n\nSubcase 2d: D is not promoted. Wait, but if D is not promoted, then D is false. So, let's see.\n\nIf D is not promoted, then D is false.\n\nFrom Diana's statement: Either E or F, but not both. So, exactly one of E or F is true.\n\nErik's statement: If E, then ¬G. So, if E is true, then G is false.\n\nFiona's statement: Either (D and G) or (¬D and ¬G). Since D is false, this simplifies to (¬D and ¬G). So, ¬D is true, so the statement is true regardless of G. So, Fiona's statement is true.\n\nGeorge's statement: Exactly two people are promoted. If D is false, then exactly two people must be promoted among E, F, G.\n\nSo, let's see.\n\nFrom Diana's statement: Exactly one of E or F is true.\n\nFrom Fiona's statement: Since D is false, Fiona's statement is automatically true.\n\nSo, promotions: Let's consider E and F.\n\nCase 2d1: E is true, F is false.\n\nSo, E is true, F is false.\n\nFrom Diana's statement: Exactly one of E or F is true. So, true.\n\nFrom Fiona's statement: Since D is false, Fiona's statement is true.\n\nFrom Erik's statement: If E is true, then ¬G. So, since E is true, G must be false.\n\nSo, G is false.\n\nSo, promotions: E is true, D is false, G is false, F is false.\n\nSo, only E is promoted. So, exactly one person is promoted. But George's statement is that exactly two are promoted. So, George is lying.\n\nSo, in this case, statements:\n\n1. Diana: True\n2. Erik: If E, then ¬G. E is true, G is false. So, implication is true. True.\n3. Fiona: True\n4. George: False\n\nSo, three statements true, one false. That works.\n\nBut wait, let's check Fiona's statement again. Fiona says either (D and G) or (¬D and ¬G). Since D is false, this is equivalent to ¬D and ¬G. So, since D is false, it's ¬D and ¬G. So, if G is false, then ¬G is true, so the statement is true.\n\nSo, in this case, E is true, D and G are false, F is false. So, only E is promoted. So, exactly one person is promoted, which is not two, so George is lying.\n\nSo, this is another valid case.\n\nBut wait, in this case, E is promoted, D and G are not. So, only E is promoted.",
