rogramming, they can analyze large datasets. So, programming implies large dataset analysis.\n\nPremise 5: Dr. Martinez specializes in neuroscience but cannot analyze large datasets. So, Dr. Martinez is in neuroscience, but he doesn't have the ability to analyze large datasets.\n\nNow, let's tackle part (a): What can we conclusively deduce about Dr. Martinez's expertise and training?\n\nFrom premise 5, we know Dr. Martinez specializes in neuroscience. From premise 1, since he specializes in neuroscience, he must have expertise in either statistics or computer programming. From premise 2, since he has expertise in either stats or programming, and premise 2 says that stats implies experimental design, so if he has stats, he must be in experimental design. But he cannot analyze large datasets, which is a different thing. Wait, does that affect anything?\n\nWait, premise 4 says that computer programming can analyze large datasets. So, if someone is in programming, they can analyze large datasets. But Dr. Martinez cannot analyze large datasets, so he cannot be in programming. Therefore, since he's in neuroscience, and he can't be in programming, he must be in statistics. Because from premise 1, he must be in stats or programming, but he can't be in programming, so he must be in stats. Therefore, he must be in stats, which means he must be in experimental design.\n\nSo, Dr. Martinez specializes in neuroscience, and he must be in experimental design. So, for part (a), we can conclude that Dr. Martinez has expertise in experimental design.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. Premise 2 says that no researcher with stats lacks experimental design, so stats implies experimental design. Premise 4 says programming implies large datasets. Premise 5 says he can't analyze large datasets, so he can't be in programming. Therefore, since he's in neuroscience, he must be in stats, which is experimental design. So, yes, he must be in experimental design.\n\nSo, part (a) answer is that Dr.