n see if the bulb lights up. But since the room is sealed, I can't actually wait and see the bulb. So, that might not work.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the fact that the bulb will only light up if the switch is flipped. But since I can only flip each switch once, I can't be sure which one is the correct one.\n\nWait, here's an idea. If I flip all three switches at the same time, but that's not allowed because I can only flip each switch once. So, I can't do that. Hmm.\n\nWait, maybe I can use the fact that flipping a switch changes its state. So, if I flip Switch A, then Switch B, then Switch C, and then check the bulb, I can see which one is the correct one based on the bulb's state. But I can only check once, so I can't do multiple flips.\n\nWait, no, I can only flip each switch once. So, I can only change the state of each switch once. So, if I flip Switch A, then Switch B, then Switch C, I can't really track which one is the correct one because each flip changes the state.\n\nWait, maybe I can use the fact that the bulb will only light up if the switch is flipped. So, if I flip Switch A, and then wait, but I can't wait because the room is sealed. So, that doesn't help.\n\nWait, maybe I can use the process of elimination. If I flip Switch A, and then Switch B, and then Switch C, and then check the bulb, I can see which one is the correct one. But I can only check once, so I can't do multiple flips.\n\nWait, no, I can only flip each switch once. So, I can only change the state of each switch once. So, if I flip Switch A, then Switch B, then Switch C, I can't really track which one is the correct one because each flip changes the state.\n\nWait, maybe I can use the fact that flipping a switch changes its state, so if I flip Switch A, then Switch B, then Switch C, and then check the bulb, I can see which one is the correct one. But I can only check once, so I can't do multiple flips.\n\nWait, I'm getting confused. Let me try to think differently.\n\nSuppose I 