    "chain": "Okay, so I have this problem where an employee needs to attend meetings A, B, C, D, E. Each meeting occurs once a week, and there are some constraints:\n\n1. They must attend at least one meeting per week.\n2. They can't attend more than three meetings.\n3. If they attend A, they must attend B.\n4. If they attend C, they can't attend D.\n5. Meeting E can only be attended if they attend exactly two other meetings.\n\nThe goal is to find all possible valid combinations of meetings they could attend, making sure they follow all these rules. Let me try to break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, let's list all the meetings: A, B, C, D, E. Each occurs once per week, so each meeting is either attended or not. But since they can attend multiple meetings, it's about combinations.\n\nConstraints:\n\n1. At least one meeting: So the combination can't be empty.\n2. At most three meetings: So the combinations can be 1, 2, or 3 meetings.\n3. If A is attended, B must be attended. So A and B can't be both not attended.\n4. If C is attended, D must not be attended. So C and D can't both be attended.\n5. E can only be attended if exactly two other meetings are attended. So E can be in a combination only if the total number of meetings in the combination is exactly three (since 2 others plus E makes three). Wait, no, hold on. If E is attended, then exactly two other meetings must be attended. So E can be in a combination only if the combination has exactly three meetings: E plus two others. But E can also be in a combination without any other constraints? Wait, no, because E can only be attended if exactly two other meetings are attended. So if E is in the combination, the total number of meetings must be three (E plus two others). If E is not in the combination, then there are no constraints on E.\n\nWait, let me clarify that. The constraint is: \"Meeting E can only be attended if you attend exactly two other meetings.\" So, if E is attended, then exactly two other meetings must be attended. If E is not attended, there's no such constraint. So E can be in a combination of size 1, 2, or 3, but if E is in the combination, the total number of meetings must be exactly three.\n\nSo, for example, if E is in the combination, then the combination must have exactly three meetings: E plus two others. If E is not in the combination, then the number of meetings can be 1, 2, or 3, but without any constraints on E.\n\nSo, the possible sizes of the combinations are 1, 2, 3, or 4? Wait, no, because if E is in the combination, it must be exactly three. So the maximum size is three. If E is not in the combination, the maximum size is three as well, but without the constraint on E. So, the possible sizes are 1, 2, 3.\n\nWait, but if E is not in the combination, can the combination have three meetings? Let me see. If E is not in the combination, then the constraints are only on A, B, C, D. So, for example, a combination of A, B, C is allowed because A requires B, and C doesn't require D. Similarly, a combination of A, C, E is not allowed because E requires exactly two other meetings, but in this case, E is in the combination, so the total number of meetings would be four, which is more than three. So, E can only be in a combination if the combination has exactly three meetings.\n\nTherefore, the possible sizes of the combinations are 1, 2, or 3. If the combination has three meetings, E must be one of them, and the other two must satisfy the constraints.\n\nSo, let's structure this.\n\nFirst, let's consider all possible combinations of size 1, 2, and 3, and check which ones satisfy all constraints.\n\nStarting with size 1:\n\n1. A: Is this allowed? If A is attended, then B must be attended. But since it's only one meeting, B isn't attended. So this violates the constraint. So A alone is invalid.\n\n2. B: Similarly, if B is attended, A must be attended. But since it's only one meeting, A isn't attended. So B alone is invalid.\n\n3. C: If C is attended, D must not be attended. So C alone is allowed because D isn't attended. So C is a valid combination.\n\n4. D: If D is attended, C must not be attended. So D alone is allowed because C isn't attended. So D is a valid combination.\n\n5. E: If E is attended, then exactly two other meetings must be attended. So E alone is invalid because it's only one meeting. So E is invalid on its own.\n\nSo, for size 1, valid combinations are C and D.\n\nNow, size 2:\n\nPossible combinations:\n\n1. A and B: Since A is attended, B must be attended. So this is allowed.\n\n2. A and C: If A is attended, B must be attended. So this is invalid because B isn't attended.\n\n3. A and D: If A is attended, B must be attended. So invalid.\n\n4. A and E: If A is attended, B must be attended. So invalid.\n\n5. B and C: If B is attended, A must be attended. So invalid.\n\n6. B and D: If B is attended, A must be attended. So invalid.\n\n7. B and E: If B is attended, A must be attended. So invalid.\n\n8. C and D: If C is attended, D must not be attended. So invalid.\n\n9. C and E: If C is attended, D must not be attended. So allowed because E is in the combination, and exactly two other meetings (C and E) are attended. So this is allowed.\n\n10. D and E: If D is attended, C must not be attended. So allowed because E is in the combination, and exactly two other meetings (D and E) are attended. So this is allowed.\n\nSo, for size 2, valid combinations are A and B, C and E, D and E.\n\nWait, let me double-check:\n\n- A and B: Valid because A requires B.\n\n- C and E: Valid because E is in the combination, and exactly two others (C and E) are attended.\n\n- D and E: Valid because E is in the combination, and exactly two others (D and E) are attended.\n\nSo, yes, those are valid.\n\nNow, size 3:\n\nPossible combinations:\n\n1. A, B, C: If A is attended, B must be attended. If C is attended, D must not be attended. So, since C is attended, D must not be. So this is invalid because D is not attended, but C is. So, this combination is invalid.\n\n2. A, B, D: If A is attended, B must be attended. If D is attended, C must not be attended. So, since D is attended, C must not be. So, this is invalid because C is not attended, but D is. So, this combination is invalid.\n\n3. A, B, E: If A is attended, B must be attended. If E is in the combination, exactly two other meetings must be attended. So, in this case, A, B, E: A and B are attended, E is attended. So, exactly three meetings, which is allowed because E is in the combination. So, this is valid.\n\n4. A, C, E: If A is attended, B must be attended. So, since A is attended, B must be. But in this combination, B is not attended. So, invalid.\n\n5. A, D, E: If A is attended, B must be. So, invalid because B isn't attended.\n\n6. B, C, E: If C is attended, D must not be. So, since C is attended, D must not be. So, this is invalid because D is not attended, but C is. So, invalid.\n\n7. B, D, E: If D is attended, C must not be. So, since D is attended, C must not be. So, this is invalid because C is not attended, but D is. So, invalid.\n\n8. C, D, E: If C is attended, D must not be. So, invalid because D is attended.\n\n9. C, E, something: Wait, we already considered C, E, and another meeting. We did C, E, and A, which is valid, and C, E, and B, which is invalid because C is attended, so D must not be. Similarly, C, E, and D is invalid because D is attended, so C must not be. So, only C, E, A is valid.\n\n10. D, E, something: D, E, and A: If D is attended, C must not be. So, since D is attended, C must not be. So, D, E, A: A is attended, so B must be. So, this is invalid because B is not attended.\n\n11. D, E, something else: D, E, and B: If D is attended, C must not be. So, invalid because C is not, but D is. So, invalid.\n\n12. E, something else: E alone is invalid. E with A: A, E is invalid because A requires B. E with B: B, E is invalid because B requires A. E with C: C, E is valid. E with D: D, E is valid. E with A, B, C