ded, D must not be attended. So C alone is allowed because D isn't attended. So C is a valid combination.\n\n4. D: If D is attended, C must not be attended. So D alone is allowed because C isn't attended. So D is a valid combination.\n\n5. E: If E is attended, then exactly two other meetings must be attended. So E alone is invalid because it's only one meeting. So E is invalid on its own.\n\nSo, for size 1, valid combinations are C and D.\n\nNow, size 2:\n\nPossible combinations:\n\n1. A and B: Since A is attended, B must be attended. So this is allowed.\n\n2. A and C: If A is attended, B must be attended. So this is invalid because B isn't attended.\n\n3. A and D: If A is attended, B must be attended. So invalid.\n\n4. A and E: If A is attended, B must be attended. So invalid.\n\n5. B and C: If B is attended, A must be attended. So invalid.\n\n6. B and D: If B is attended, A must be attended. So invalid.\n\n7. B and E: If B is attended, A must be attended. So invalid.\n\n8. C and D: If C is attended, D must not be attended. So invalid.\n\n9. C and E: If C is attended, D must not be attended. So allowed because E is in the combination, and exactly two other meetings (C and E) are attended. So this is allowed.\n\n10. D and E: If D is attended, C must not be attended. So allowed because E is in the combination, and exactly two other meetings (D and E) are attended. So this is allowed.\n\nSo, for size 2, valid combinations are A and B, C and E, D and E.\n\nWait, let me double-check:\n\n- A and B: Valid because A requires B.\n\n- C and E: Valid because E is in the combination, and exactly two others (C and E) are attended.\n\n- D and E: Valid because E is in the combination, and exactly two others (D and E) are attended.\n\nSo, yes, those are valid.\n\nNow, size 3:\n\nPossible combinations:\n\n1. A, B, C: If A is attended, B must be attended. If C is attended, D must not be attended. So, since C is attended, D must not be. So this is invalid because D is not attended, but C is. So, this combination is invalid.\n\n2. A, B, D: If A is attended, B must be attended. If D is attended, C must not be attended. So, since D is attended, C must not be. So, this is invalid because C is not attended, but D is. So, this combination is invalid.\n\n3. A, B, E: If A is attended, B must be attended. If E is in the combination, exactly two other meetings must be attended. So, in this case, A, B, E: A and B are attended, E is attended. So, exactly three meetings, which is allowed because E is in the combination. So, this is valid.\n\n4. A, C, E: If A is attended, B must be attended. So, since A is attended, B must be. But in this combination, B is not attended. So, invalid.\n\n5. A, D, E: If A is attended, B must be. So, invalid because B isn't attended.\n\n6. B, C, E: If C is attended, D must not be. So, since C is attended, D must not be. So, this is invalid because D is not attended, but C is. So, invalid.\n\n7. B, D, E: If D is attended, C must not be. So, since D is attended, C must not be. So, this is invalid because C is not attended, but D is. So, invalid.\n\n8. C, D, E: If C is attended, D must not be. So, invalid because D is attended.\n\n9. C, E, something: Wait, we already considered C, E, and another meeting. We did C, E, and A, which is valid, and C, E, and B, which is invalid because C is attended, so D must not be. Similarly, C, E, and D is invalid because D is attended, so C must not be. So, only C, E, A is valid.\n\n10. D, E, something: D, E, and A: If D is attended, C must not be. So, since D is attended, C must not be. So, D, E, A: A is attended, so B must be. So, this is invalid because B is not attended.\n\n11. D, E, something else: D, E, and B: If D is attended, C must not be. So, invalid because C is not, but D is. So, invalid.\n\n12. E, something else: E alone is invalid. E with A: A, E is invalid because A requires B. E with B: B, E is invalid because B requires A. E with C: C, E is valid. E with D: D, E is valid. E with A, B, C: A, B, E is valid. E with A, D, E: D is in the combination, so C must not be. So, invalid.\n\nSo, for size 3, the valid combinations are:\n\n- A, B, E\n\n- C, E\n\n- D, E\n\nWait, hold on. Let me check again.\n\nWait, in combination C, E: C is attended, E is attended, so exactly two others (C and E) are attended. So, that's allowed.\n\nSimilarly, D, E: D is attended, E is attended, so exactly two others (D and E) are attended. So, allowed.\n\nA, B, E: A is attended, so B must be. B is attended, E is. So, exactly three meetings, which is allowed because E is in the combination.\n\nWait, but in combination A, B, E, E is in the combination, so exactly two others (A and B) are attended. So, that's allowed.\n\nSo, yes, A, B, E is valid.\n\nSo, for size 3, valid combinations are:\n\n- A, B, E\n\n- C, E\n\n- D, E\n\nWait, but hold on. Let me check if E can be in a combination of three. So, E is in the combination, so exactly two others must be attended. So, in combination A, B, E: A and B are two others, so that's allowed.\n\nSimilarly, C, E: exactly two others (C and E), but wait, no, C is one, E is one, so two others. Wait, no, if E is in the combination, the other two must be attended. So, in C, E, someone else: C and E are two, so the third must be someone else. So, A, C, E is invalid because A is in the combination, so B must be. So, A, C, E is invalid.\n\nSimilarly, D, E, someone else: D and E are two, so the third must be someone else. So, A, D, E is invalid because A requires B. B, D, E is invalid because B requires A. So, only A, B, E is valid.\n\nSo, yes, only A, B, E is valid for size 3.\n\nWait, but hold on. Let me think again. If E is in the combination, the other two must be attended. So, for example, if I have A, B, E: A and B are two others, so that's allowed.\n\nSimilarly, C, E: C is one, E is one, so two others. So, that's allowed.\n\nSimilarly, D, E: D is one, E is one, so two others. So, that's allowed.\n\nBut if I have A, C, E: A is in the combination, so B must be. So, A, C, E is invalid because B isn't.\n\nSimilarly, A, D, E: A requires B, so invalid.\n\nSo, only A, B, E is valid for size 3.\n\nSo, summarizing:\n\nSize 1: C, D\n\nSize 2: A, B; C, E; D, E\n\nSize 3: A, B, E\n\nWait, but hold on. Let me check if there are any other combinations.\n\nWait, for size 3, is there another combination? Let's see.\n\nWhat about B, C, E: If C is attended, D must not be. So, since C is attended, D must not be. So, B, C, E: D is not attended, so that's allowed. Wait, but in this case, C is attended, so D must not be. So, B, C, E is allowed because D is not. So, is that a valid combination?\n\nWait, hold on. If I have B, C, E: C is attended, so D must not be. So, D is not in the combination, so that's allowed. So, B, C, E is a valid combination.\n\nWait, but earlier, I thought that C, E is valid, but B, C, E is also valid. So, perhaps I missed that.\n\nSimilarly, A, C, E: A is in the combination, so B must be. So, A, C, E is invalid.\n\nSimilarly, B, D, E: If D is attended, C must not be. So, since D is in the combination, C must not be. So, B, D, E: C is not in the combination, so that's allowed. So, B, D, E is a valid combination.\n\nWait, so I think I missed that earlier.\n\nSo, let me re-examine size 3.\n\nPossible combinations:\n\n1. A, B, C: Invalid because C is in the combination, so D must not be. But D is not in the combination, so that's okay? Wait, no, because if C is in the combination, D must not be. So, in A, B, C: C is in, so D must not be. But D is not in the combination, so that's okay. Wait, but A, B, C: A requires B, which is in, so that's okay. C requires D, which is not in, so that's okay. So, A, B, C is allowed.\n\nWait, hold on. So, A, B, C is allowed because:\n\n- A is in, so B is in.\n\n- C is in, so D is not in.\n\nSo, that's allowed.\n\nSimilarly, A, B, D: Invalid because D is in, so C must not be. But C is not in, so that's okay? Wait, no, because if D is in, C must not be. So, A, B, D: D is in, so C must not be. But C is not in, so that's okay. Wait, but A, B, D: A requires B, which is in. D requires C, which is not in. So, that's allowed.\n\nWait, so A, B, D is allowed.\n\nWait, so I think I made a mistake earlier. Let me go through all size 3 combinations again.\n\n1. A, B, C: Valid.\n\n2. A, B, D: Valid.\n\n3. A, B, E: Valid.\n\n4. A, C, E: Invalid (because A requires B).\n\n5. A, D, E: Invalid (because A requires B).\n\n6. B, C, E: Valid.\n\n7. B, D, E: Valid.\n\n8. C, D, E: Invalid (because C requires D).\n\n9. C, E, something: C, E, A: Valid.\n\n10. C, E, B: Invalid (because C requires D).\n\n11. C, E, D: Invalid (because C requires D).\n\n12. D, E, something: D, E, A: Invalid (because D requires C).\n\n13. D, E, B: Invalid (because D requires C).\n\n14. D, E, C: Invalid (because C requires D).\n\n15. E, something else: E alone is invalid.\n\nSo, the valid size 3 combinations are:\n\n- A, B, C\n\n- A, B, D\n\n- A, B, E\n\n- B, C, E\n\n- B, D, E\n\n- C, E, A\n\nWait, but C, E, A is same as A, C, E, which is invalid because A requires B. So, only A, B, C and A, B, D are valid.\n\nWait, no, A, B, C is valid because C is in, so D is not. A, B, D is valid because D is in, so C is not. So, both are valid.\n\nSimilarly, B, C, E is valid because C is in, so D is not. B, D, E is valid because D is in, so C is not.\n\nSo, in total, size 3 combinations are:\n\n- A, B, C\n\n- A, B, D\n\n- B, C, E\n\n- B, D, E\n\nSo, four size 3 combinations.\n\nWait, but earlier I thought A, B, E was valid, but now I see that A, B, C and A, B, D are also valid.\n\nSo, in total, size 3: A, B, C; A, B, D; B, C, E; B, D, E.\n\nSo, that's four combinations.\n\nWait, but let me check each one:\n\n1. A, B, C: Valid.\n\n2. A, B, D: Valid.\n\n3. B, C, E: Valid.\n\n4. B, D, E: Valid.\n\nSo, yes, four size 3 combinations.\n\nSo, summarizing all valid combinations:\n\nSize 1:\n\n- C\n\n- D\n\nSize 2:\n\n- A, B\n\n- C, E\n\n- D, E\n\nSize 3:\n\n- A, B, C\n\n- A, B, D\n\n- B, C, E\n\n- 