eir productivity stayed the same.\n\n10% saw a negative change, so their productivity decreased by some amount, say, -y.\n\nSo, the overall productivity after the policy would be:\n\n0.6*(P + x) + 0.3*P + 0.1*(P - y) = 0.6P + 0.6x + 0.3P + 0.1P - 0.1y = (0.6 + 0.3 + 0.1)P + 0.6x - 0.1y = P + 0.6x - 0.1y.\n\nNow, the overall productivity went up, so P + 0.6x - 0.1y > P, which implies that 0.6x - 0.1y > 0, or 0.6x > 0.1y, or 6x > y.\n\nSo, the increase in productivity of the 60% (x) must be more than the decrease in productivity of the 10% (y) by a factor of 10.\n\nWait, that seems possible. So, if the 60% who saw a positive change had a productivity increase of, say, 10%, and the 10% who saw a negative change had a productivity decrease of 6%, then the overall productivity would go up.\n\nBut in the problem, it's stated that 60% saw a positive change, 30% maintained the same, and 10% saw a negative change. So, the overall productivity went up beyond the levels seen during the flexible policy period, even though 60% of employees had performed better under flexibility.\n\nWait, but in this scenario, the 60% who saw a positive change were the ones who were the most productive, and the 30% who saw no change were the ones who were less productive, but the overall productivity went up because the 60% who saw a positive change were the ones who were the most productive, and the 30% who saw no change were the ones who were less productive, but the overall average went up because the 60% who saw a positive change were the ones who were the most productive.\n\nWait, perhaps the key is that the 60% who saw a positive change were the ones who were the most productive, and the 30% who saw no change were the ones who were less productive, but the overall productivity went up because the 60% who saw a positive change were the ones who were the most productive, and the 30% who saw no change were the ones who were less productive, but the overall average went up because the 60% who s