    "chain": "Okay, so I have this problem where I'm rolling a fair six-sided die repeatedly, and I win if I roll a 6 before I roll two consecutive odd numbers. I need to figure out a strategy and the probability of winning. Hmm, let me think about how to approach this.\n\nFirst, let me try to understand the problem. I'm rolling a die, and each roll is independent, right? The die is fair, so each number from 1 to 6 has an equal probability of 1/6. The game ends when I either roll a 6 or when I roll two consecutive odd numbers. So, if I roll a 6, I win; if I roll two odds in a row, I lose. If I roll an even number, I continue rolling.\n\nI need to figure out the probability that I win before I lose. So, I should probably model this as a Markov process with states representing the current situation. Let me try to define the states.\n\nLet me think about the possible states I can be in:\n\n1. Start state: No previous roll, or the last roll was even. Wait, actually, maybe I need to consider the last roll's parity because the losing condition is two consecutive odds. So, perhaps the states should represent the number of consecutive odd numbers rolled so far.\n\nWait, but the losing condition is two consecutive odds, so maybe the states can be:\n\n- State S: Start state, no previous odd number.\n- State O: One odd number rolled.\n- State Win: Win state, which is when I roll a 6.\n- State Lose: Lose state, which is when I roll two consecutive odds.\n\nBut actually, the game can also end when I roll a 6, so maybe I need to include that as a separate state.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I can model the states based on the number of consecutive odd numbers I've rolled. So, the states would be:\n\n- S: Start state, no previous odd number.\n- O: One odd number rolled.\n- OO: Two consecutive odd numbers, which is a losing state.\n- Win: Winning state, which is when I roll a 6.\n\nBut wait, in the problem statement, it's two consecutive odd numbers, so rolling an odd number after ano