      {
        "label": "deduction",
        "text": "So, to apply Bayes' Theorem, I need P(forecast rain | rain) and P(rain). But I also need P(forecast rain), which is the total probability that the forecast predicts rain on any given day, regardless of whether it actually rains or not."
      },
      {
        "label": "adding-knowledge",
        "text": "To find P(forecast rain), I can use the law of total probability. That is:\n\nP(forecast rain) = P(forecast rain | rain) * P(rain) + P(forecast rain | no rain) * P(no rain)"
      },
      {
        "label": "deduction",
        "text": "Plugging in the numbers:\n\nP(forecast rain) = (0.70 * 0.25) + (0.20 * 0.75)\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n0.70 * 0.25 = 0.175\n\n0.20 * 0.75 = 0.15\n\nAdding them together: 0.175 + 0.15 = 0.325\n\nSo, P(forecast rain) = 0.325"
      },
      {
        "label": "deduction",
        "text": "Now, applying Bayes' Theorem:\n\nP(rain | forecast rain) = [P(forecast rain | rain) * P(rain)] / P(forecast rain)\n\nPlugging in the numbers:\n\nP(rain | forecast rain) = (0.70 * 0.25) / 0.325\n\nCalculating the numerator: 0.70 * 0.25 = 0.175\n\nSo, 0.175 / 0.325. Let me compute that."
