t. We can find P(C | A) using Bayes' theorem.\n\nP(C | A) = P(A | C) * P(C) / P(A)\n\nWe know P(A | C) = 0.75, P(C) = 0.08, and P(A) = 0.60.\n\nSo, P(C | A) = (0.75 * 0.08) / 0.60 = (0.06) / 0.60 = 0.10.\n\nSimilarly, P(Not C | A) = 1 - P(C | A) = 0.90.\n\nSo, P(T+ | A) = P(T+ | C) * P(C | A) + P(T+ | Not C) * P(Not C | A) = 0.90 * 0.10 + 0.05 * 0.90 = 0.09 + 0.045 = 0.135.\n\nSo, P(T+ | A) = 0.135.\n\nSimilarly, P(T- | A) = 1 - 0.135 = 0.865.\n\nNow, we can find P(T+ and A) = P(T+ | A) * P(A) = 0.135 * 0.60 = 0.081.\n\nSimilarly, P(C and A) = P(C | A) * P(A) = 0.10 * 0.60 = 0.06.\n\nTherefore, P(C | T+, A) = P(C and A) / P(T+, A) = 0.06 / 0.081 ≈ 0.7407.\n\nWait, so that's approximately 74.07%.\n\nBut wait, that seems high. Let me check my calculations.\n\nFirst, P(C | A) = (0.75 * 0.08) / 0.60 = 0.06 / 0.60 = 0.10. That seems correct.\n\nThen, P(T+ | A) = 0.90 * 0.10 + 0.05 * 0.90 = 0.09 + 0.045 = 0.135. That also seems correct.\n\nThen, P(T+ and A) = 0.135 * 0.60 = 0.081.\n\nP(C and A) = 0.10 * 0.60 = 0.06.\n\nTherefore, P(C | T+, A) = 0.06 / 0.081 ≈ 0.7407, which is approximately 74.07%.\n\nHmm, that seems correct. So, the probability is about 74.07%.\n\nWait, but let me think again. If someone has variant A and tested positive, what's the chance they have the condition? It's 74%, which is higher than the overall prevalence of 8%. That makes sense because variant A is a marker, so having variant A increases the likelihood of having the condition.\n\nBut let me think about the false positive rate. The false positive rate is 5%, which is the probability of testing positive when you don't have the condition. So, if someone has variant A but doesn't have the condition, they have a 5% chance of testing positive. But in this case, since we're given that they tested positive and have variant A, the 5% false positive rate doesn't directly affect the probability because we're already in the positive and A category.\n\nWait, but in the calculation above, we used Bayes' th