    "chain": "Okay, so I have this project management problem to solve. There are four independent tasks, each assigned to a different team member. Each task has a certain probability of being completed on time based on historical data:\n\n- Task 1: 90% probability\n- Task 2: 85% probability\n- Task 3: 75% probability\n- Task 4: 80% probability\n\nThe project succeeds if at least three of the four tasks are completed on time. I need to find two things:\n\n1. The probability that the project succeeds as it is.\n2. If I can allocate additional resources to improve the on-time probability of exactly one task by 15 percentage points, which task should I choose to maximize the project success probability, and what would the new success probability be?\n\nAlright, let's tackle the first part first.\n\n**Part 1: Probability the project succeeds**\n\nThe project succeeds if at least three tasks are completed on time. Since there are four tasks, the project can succeed in two scenarios:\n\n1. Exactly three tasks are completed on time.\n2. All four tasks are completed on time.\n\nSince the tasks are independent, I can model this using the binomial probability formula. The binomial probability formula is:\n\n\\[ P(k) = C(n, k) \\times p^k \\times (1-p)^{n-k} \\]\n\nWhere:\n- \\( C(n, k) \\) is the combination of n things taken k at a time.\n- \\( p \\) is the probability of success for a single task.\n- \\( k \\) is the number of successes.\n\nBut wait, in this case, each task has a different probability of success. So, it's not a standard binomial distribution because each trial (task) has a different probability. That complicates things a bit.\n\nHmm, so I need to calculate the probability of exactly three tasks succeeding and the probability of all four tasks succeeding, then add them together.\n\nLet me denote the tasks as A, B, C, D with probabilities 0.9, 0.85, 0.75, 0.8 respectively.\n\nFirst, let's compute the probability of exactly three tasks succeeding.\n\nThere are four possible combinations for exactly three tasks succeeding:\n\n1. A, B, C succeed; D fails.\n2. A, B, D succeed; C fails.\n3. A, C, D succeed; B fails.\n4. B, C, D succeed; A fails.\n\nEach of these combinations is mutually exclusive, so I can compute each probability and sum them up.\n\nLet's compute each one:\n\n1. A, B, C succeed; D fails:\n   - Probability = 0.9 * 0.85 * 0.75 * (1 - 0.8) = 0.9 * 0.85 * 0.75 * 0.2\n   - Let me compute that: 0.9 * 0.85 = 0.765; 0.765 * 0.75 = 0.57375; 0.57375 * 0.2 = 0.11475\n\n2. A, B, D succeed; C fails:\n   - Probability = 0.9 * 0.85 * (1 - 0.75) * 0.8 = 0.9 * 0.85 * 0.25 * 0.8\n   - Compute step by step: 0.9 * 0.85 = 0.765; 0.765 * 0.25 = 0.19125; 0.19125 * 0.8 = 0.153\n\n3. A, C, D succeed; B fails:\n   - Probability = 0.9 * (1 - 0.85) * 0.8 * 0.8 = 0.9 * 0.15 * 0.8 * 0.8\n   - Compute: 0.9 * 0.15 = 0.135; 0.135 * 0.8 = 0.108; 0.108 * 0.8 = 0.0864\n\n4. B, C, D succeed; A fails:\n   - Probability = (1 - 0.9) * 0.85 * 0.8 * 0.8 = 0.1 * 0.85 * 0.8 * 0.8\n   - Compute: 0.1 * 0.85 = 0.085; 0.085 * 0.8 = 0.068; 0.068 * 0.8 = 0.0544\n\nNow, sum all these probabilities:\n\n0.11475 + 0.153 + 0.0864 + 0.0544\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\n0.11475 + 0.153 = 0.26775\n\n0.26775 + 0.0864 = 0.35415\n\n0.35415 + 0.0544 = 0.40855\n\nSo, the probability of exactly three tasks succeeding is 0.40855 or 40.855%.\n\nNext, compute the probability that all four tasks succeed:\n\n- Probability = 0.9 * 0.85 * 0.75 * 0.8\n\nCompute step by step:\n\n0.9 * 0.85 = 0.765\n\n0.765 * 0.75 = 0.57375\n\n0.57375 * 0.8 = 0.459\n\nSo, the probability of all four tasks succeeding is 0.459 or 45.9%.\n\nTherefore, the total probability that the project succeeds is the sum of the two:\n\n0.40855 + 0.459 = 0.86755\n\nSo, approximately 86.755% chance.\n\nWait, let me double-check my calculations to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nFirst, for exactly three tasks:\n\n1. A, B, C succeed; D fails: 0.9 * 0.85 * 0.75 * 0.2 = 0.11475\n2. A, B, D succeed; C fails: 0.9 * 0.85 * 0.25 * 0.8 = 0.153\n3. A, C, D succeed; B fails: 0.9 * 0.15 * 0.8 * 0.8 = 0.0864\n4. B, C, D succeed; A fails: 0.1 * 0.85 * 0.8 * 0.8 = 0.0544\n\nAdding them up: 0.11475 + 0.153 = 0.26775; 0.26775 + 0.0864 = 0.35415; 0.35415 + 0.0544 = 0.40855\n\nAll four tasks: 0.9 * 0.85 * 0.75 * 0.8 = 0.459\n\nTotal: 0.40855 + 0.459 = 0.86755\n\nYes, that seems correct.\n\nSo, the probability that the project succeeds is approximately 86.755%, or 0.86755.\n\n**Part 2: Maximizing the project success probability by allocating additional resources to one task**\n\nNow, the second part is more complex. We can allocate additional resources to exactly one task, increasing its on-time probability by 15 percentage points. So, each task's probability will increase by 15%, but we can only do this for one task. We need to determine which task to allocate resources to in order to maximize the project success probability.\n\nFirst, let's note the current probabilities:\n\n- Task 1: 90% (0.9)\n- Task 2: 85% (0.85)\n- Task 3: 75% (0.75)\n- Task 4: 80% (0.8)\n\nIf we allocate resources to one task, its probability will increase by 15%, so:\n\n- Task 1: 90% + 15% = 105%? Wait, that can't be right. Probabilities can't exceed 100%. So, perhaps it's 90% + 15% = 105%, but that's impossible. So, maybe it's 90% + 15% = 105%, but since probabilities can't exceed 100%, perhaps it's capped at 100%. Alternatively, maybe the increase is 15 percentage points, so 90% + 15% = 105%, but since probabilities can't exceed 100%, perhaps it's 100%. Hmm, that seems odd.\n\nWait, maybe the problem is that the increase is 15 percentage points, so if a task has a probability of p, it becomes p + 15, but if p + 15 > 100, then it's 100. So, let's recast the probabilities:\n\n- Task 1: 90% + 15% = 105% → 100%\n- Task 2: 85% + 15% = 100%\n- Task 3: 75% + 15% = 90%\n- Task 4: 80% + 15% = 95%\n\nAlternatively, maybe it's 15 percentage points, so 90% + 15% = 105%, but since probabilities can't exceed 100%, perhaps it's 100%. Similarly, Task 2: 85% + 15% = 100%, Task 3: 75% + 15% = 90%, Task 4: 80% + 15% = 95%.\n\nWait, but that seems a bit odd because increasing a task's probability beyond 100% is not possible. So, perhaps the increase is 15 percentage points, but if it exceeds 100%, it's capped at 100%. So, let's adjust accordingly.\n\nSo, the new probabilities after allocating resources to each task would be:\n\n1. Allocate to Task 1: 100% (since 90% + 15% = 105%, capped at 100%)\n2. Allocate to Task 2: 100% (85% + 15% = 100%)\n3. Allocate to Task 3: 90% (75% + 15% = 90%)\n4. Allocate to Task 4: 95% (80% + 15% = 95%)\n\nNow, for each of these scenarios, we need to compute the probability that the project succeeds, which is the probability of at least three tasks succeeding.\n\nSo, let's compute the success probability for each case.\n\n**Case 1: Allocate to Task 1 (now 100% success)**\n- Task 1: 100% success\n- Tasks 2, 3, 4: 85%, 75%, 80% respectively\n\nWe need at least three tasks to succeed. Since Task 1 is always successful, we need at least two more tasks to succeed among Tasks 2, 3, 4.\n\nSo, the number of successes needed is 2 or 3.\n\nCompute the probability of exactly two succeeding and exactly three succeeding.\n\nBut since we have three tasks, the probability of exactly two succeeding is:\n\nC(3,2) * (0.85)^2 * (0.75)^1 * (0.80)^0\n\nWait, no. Wait, actually, the probabilities are:\n\n- Task 2: 85% (0.85)\n- Task 3: 75% (0.75)\n- Task 4: 80% (0.8)\n\nSo, the probability of exactly two succeeding is:\n\nC(3,2) * (0.85)^2 * (0.75)^1 * (0.8)^0\n\nWait, no, that's not correct. Because for exactly two successes, we have to choose two tasks that succeed and one that fails.\n\nSo, the probability is:\n\nC(3,2) * (0.85)^2 * (1 - 0.85)^1 * (1 - 0.75)^0 * (1 - 0.8)^0\n\nWait, no, that's not correct either. Wait, actually, for exactly two successes, it's:\n\nC(3,2) * (0.85)^2 * (0.75)^1 * (0.8)^0\n\nBut wait, no, that's not correct because the probabilities are for eac