    "chain": "Okay, so I have this problem about a casino game where I flip a biased coin. The coin lands heads 60% of the time and tails 40%. I start with $100, and each round I bet $10. If it's heads, I win $10, and if it's tails, I lose $10. The game ends when I either reach $150 (win) or go broke to $0 (loss). I need to find two things: the probability that I reach $150 before going broke, and the expected number of rounds until the game ends.\n\nHmm, this sounds like a classic gambler's ruin problem. I remember that in gambler's ruin, you have a gambler with finite wealth who bets on a game with probability p of winning and q=1-p of losing. The goal is to find the probability of reaching a certain wealth before going bankrupt.\n\nIn this case, the gambler starts with $100, and the target is $150. So, the total possible wealth is from $0 to $150. The probability of winning each bet is p=0.6, and losing is q=0.4.\n\nFirst, I need to recall the formula for the probability of reaching a certain wealth before ruin in a gambler's ruin problem. I think it's something like:\n\nIf the gambler starts with i dollars, the probability of reaching N before 0 is:\n\nP(i) = [1 - (q/p)^i] / [1 - (q/p)^N]\n\nBut wait, this formula is when p ≠ q. If p = q, then the probability is i/N. But in our case, p=0.6 and q=0.4, so p ≠ q.\n\nSo, plugging in the values, N is 150, and i is 100. So,\n\nP(100) = [1 - (0.4/0.6)^100] / [1 - (0.4/0.6)^150]\n\nLet me compute (0.4/0.6). That's 2/3, approximately 0.6667.\n\nSo, (2/3)^100 is a very small number, since 2/3 is less than 1, and raising it to the 100th power makes it extremely small. Similarly, (2/3)^150 is even smaller.\n\nTherefore, 1 - (2/3)^100 is approximately 1, and 1 - (2/3)^150 is also approximately 1. So, the probability P(100) is approximately 1 / 1 = 1. Wait, that can't be right. If p > q, the probability of reaching N before ruin should be higher than 0.5, right?\n\nWait, maybe I got the formula wrong. Let me double-check.\n\nI think the correct formula is:\n\nIf p ≠ q, then P(i) = [1 - (q/p)^i] / [1 - (q/p)^N]\n\nBut if p > q, then (q/p) < 1, so (q/p)^i is less than 1, so 1 - (q/p)^i is positive, and 1 - (q/p)^N is also positive, so P(i) is positive.\n\nBut in our case, (2/3)^100 is a very small number, so 1 - (2/3)^100 is approximately 1, and 1 - (2/3)^150 is also approximately 1. So, P(100) is approximately 1 / 1 = 1. That suggests that the probability of reaching $150 before going broke is almost 1, which seems counterintuitive because the coin is biased towards tails.\n\nWait, maybe I have the formula backwards. Let me think again.\n\nIn gambler's ruin, the probability of reaching N before 0 starting from i is:\n\nP(i) = [1 - (q/p)^i] / [1 - (q/p)^N]\n\nBut if p > q, then (q/p) < 1, so (q/p)^i decreases as i increases. So, if I start at i=100, which is less than N=150, the probability should be higher than 0.5, but according to the formula, it's [1 - (q/p)^100] / [1 - (q/p)^150]. Since (q/p)^100 is much smaller than (q/p)^150, the numerator is approximately 1 - 0, and the denominator is approximately 1 - 0, so P(100) is approximately 1. That seems odd.\n\nWait, maybe I should think about it differently. If p > q, the probability of reaching N before ruin is higher than the probability of ruin. But in this case, since p=0.6 and q=0.4, the probability of ruin is (q/p)^N, which is (0.4/0.6)^150, which is a very small number, so the probability of reaching N is 1 - (q/p)^N, which is almost 1.\n\nBut that seems to contradict the intuition that since the coin is biased towards tails, the probability of ruin is higher. Wait, no, actually, if p > q, the probability of ruin is (q/p)^N, which is less than 1, so the probability of reaching N is 1 - (q/p)^N, which is greater than 1 - (q/p)^N. Wait, no, that's not correct.\n\nWait, let me recall the formula correctly. The probability of ruin is (q/p)^i, but I think it's actually (q/p)^i, but I might be mixing up the formula.\n\nWait, no, let me look it up in my mind. The probability of ruin in gambler's ruin is (q/p)^i, but only if p ≠ q. Wait, no, that can't be because if p=1, then ruin probability is 0, which is (q/p)^i = 0^i = 0, which is correct. If p=0, ruin probability is 1, which is (q/p)^i, but that's undefined. So, maybe the formula is different.\n\nWait, perhaps the probability of ruin is (q/p)^i, but if p > q, then (q/p)^i is less than 1, so the probability of ruin is less than 1, meaning the probability of reaching N is 1 - (q/p)^i.\n\nWait, that seems to make sense. So, if p > q, the probability of ruin is (q/p)^i, so the probability of reaching N is 1 - (q/p)^i.\n\nSo, in our case, P(100) = 1 - (0.4/0.6)^100.\n\nCompute (0.4/0.6)^100 = (2/3)^100.\n\nCompute (2/3)^100. Let's compute ln(2/3) = ln(0.6667) ≈ -0.4055. Multiply by 100: -40.55. Exponentiate: e^(-40.55) ≈ 1.3e-18. So, (2/3)^100 ≈ 1.3e-18.\n\nTherefore, P(100) ≈ 1 - 1.3e-18 ≈ 1. So, practically 1. So, the probability of reaching $150 is almost 1, and the probability of ruin is almost 0.\n\nBut that seems counterintuitive because the coin is biased towards tails, so the probability of ruin is higher. Wait, no, actually, if p > q, the probability of ruin is (q/p)^i, which is less than 1, so the probability of reaching N is 1 - (q/p)^i, which is greater than 1 - 1 = 0, but in this case, it's 1 - something very small, so it's almost 1.\n\nWait, but intuitively, if the coin is biased towards tails, shouldn't the probability of ruin be higher? Hmm, maybe not. Because even though the coin is biased towards tails, the starting point is closer to the target, so the probability of ruin is still low.\n\nWait, let's think about it. If I start at $100, and the target is $150, so I have 50 dollars to go to win. The probability of winning each round is 0.6, so it's more likely to win, so the probability of ruin is low.\n\nBut if I started at $100, and the target was $50, then the probability of ruin would be higher. So, in this case, since the target is $150, which is 50 dollars away, and I start at $100, which is 50 dollars away from ruin, the probability of ruin is (0.4/0.6)^50 ≈ (2/3)^50 ≈ 1e-10, which is extremely low. So, the probability of ruin is practically 0, and the probability of reaching $150 is almost 1.\n\nWait, that seems correct. Because even though the coin is biased towards tails, the starting point is closer to the target, so the probability of ruin is low.\n\nSo, the probability of reaching $150 is approximately 1, and the probability of ruin is approximately 0.\n\nBut let me verify this with another approach. Let's model this as a Markov chain. The states are the bankroll amounts from $0 to $150. The states $0 and $150 are absorbing. All other states are transient.\n\nThe transition probabilities: from state i, with probability 0.6, go to i+10, and with probability 0.4, go to i-10.\n\nWe can set up equations for the probability of reaching $150 before $0 starting from each state i.\n\nLet P(i) be the probability of reaching $150 before $0 starting from i.\n\nWe have boundary conditions: P(0) = 0, P(150) = 1.\n\nFor 0 < i < 150, P(i) = 0.6 * P(i+10) + 0.4 * P(i-10).\n\nThis is a linear recurrence relation. The general solution is P(i) = A + B*(q/p)^i.\n\nGiven that q/p = 0.4/0.6 = 2/3.\n\nSo, P(i) = A + B*(2/3)^i.\n\nApply boundary conditions:\n\nAt i=0: P(0) = A + B*(2/3)^0 = A + B = 0.\n\nAt i=150: P(150) = A + B*(2/3)^150 = 1.\n\nFrom the first equation: A = -B.\n\nSubstitute into the second equation:\n\n-A - B*(2/3)^150 = 1.\n\nBut A = -B, so:\n\n-(-B) - B*(2/3)^150 = 1 => B - B*(2/3)^150 = 1.\n\nFactor out B:\n\nB[1 - (2/3)^150] = 1.\n\nTherefore, B = 1 / [1 - (2/3)^150].\n\nThen, A = -B = -1 / [1 - (2/3)^150].\n\nTherefore, P(i) = [1 - (2/3)^i] / [1 - (2/3)^150].\n\nSo, for i=100:\n\nP(100) = [1 - (2/3)^100] / [1 - (2/3)^150].\n\nWhich is exactly the formula I had earlier.\n\nSo, as (2/3)^100 is approximately 1.3e-18, and (2/3)^150 is approximately 1.3e-20, so both are negligible.\n\nTherefore, P(100) ≈ 1 / (1 - 0) = 1.\n\nSo, the probability of reaching $150 is approximately 1, and the probability of ruin is approximately 0.\n\nWait, but that seems counterintuitive because the coin is biased towards tails, so shouldn't the probability of ruin be higher? But in this case, the starting point is closer to the target, so the probability of ruin is low.\n\nWait, let me think again. If I start at $100, which is 50 dollars away from ruin and 50 dollars away from winning. The probability of ruin is (q/p)^50 = (0.4/0.6)^50 ≈ (2/3)^50 ≈ 1e-10, which is extremely low. So, the probability of ruin is practically 0, and the probability of reaching $150 is practically 1.\n\nSo, that seems correct.\n\nTherefore, the probability of reaching $150 is approximately 1, and the probability of ruin is approximately 0.\n\nNow, moving on to the second part: the expected number of rounds until the game ends.\n\nIn gambler's ruin, the expected number of steps can be calculated using the formula:\n\nE = (N - i) / (p - q)\n\nBut wait, that formula is when p ≠ q. If p = q, then the expected number of steps is infinite, which is the case when the game is fair.\n\nIn our case, p=0.6 and q=0.4, so p ≠ q. Therefore, the expected number of steps is:\n\nE = (150 - 100) / (0.6 - 0.4) = 50 / 0.2 = 250.\n\nWait, that seems straightforward. So, the expected number of rounds is 250.\n\nBut let me verify this formula. The expected number of steps in gambler's ruin is indeed (N - i)/(p - q) when p ≠ q.\n\nSo, plugging in N=150, i=100, p=0.6, q=0.4:\n\nE = (150 - 100)/(0.6 - 0.4) = 50 / 0.2 = 250.\n\nSo, the expected number of rounds is 250.\n\nBut wait, let me think about this. The formula assumes that the game is fair or unfair, but in our case, it's unfair. So, the expected number of steps is finite, which makes sense because the game will almost surely end.\n\nAlternatively, we can derive the expected number of steps using the recurrence relation.\n\nLet E(i) be the expected number of steps starting from i.\n\nWe have E(0) = 0, E(150) = 0.\n\nFor 0 < i < 150:\n\nE(i) = 1 + 0.6 * E(i+10) + 0.4 * E(i-10)\n\nThis is a linear recurrence relation. The solution is similar to the probability solution.\n\nThe general solution is E(i) = A + B*(q/p)^i.\n\nApplying boundary conditions:\n\nE(0) = A + B = 0 => A = -B.\n\nE(150) = A + B*(q/p)^150 = 0 => -B + B*(q/p)^150 = 0 => B[(q/p)^150 - 1] = 0.\n\nSince (q/p)^150 ≠ 1, we have B = 0, which implies A = 0.\n\nWait, that can't be right because then E(i) = 0 for all i, which is not possible.\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in setting up the recurrence.\n\nWait, actually, in gambler's ruin, the expected number of steps is different. The formula I used earlier is for the probability, not the expected number of steps.\n\nWait, let me recall. The expected number of steps can be found by solving the recurrence relation.\n\nLet me denote E(i) as the expected number of steps starting from i.\n\nWe have E(0) = 0, E(150) = 0.\n\nFor 0 < i < 150:\n\nE(i) = 1 + 0.6 * E(i+10) + 0.4 * E(i-10)\n\nThis is a linear difference equation. The solution is E(i) = A + B*(q/p)^i.\n\nBut applying the boundary conditions:\n\nAt i=0: E(0) = A + B = 0 => A = -B.\n\nAt i=150: E(150) = A + B*(q/p)^150 = 0.\n\nSubstituting A = -B:\n\n-B + B*(q/p)^150 = 0 => B[(q/p)^150 - 1] = 0.\n\nSince (q/p)^150 ≠ 1, we have B = 0, which implies A = 0.\n\nBut that would mean E(i) = 0 for all i, which is impossible. So, perhaps I need to consider a different approach.\n\nWait, maybe the formula for expected number of steps is different. Let me recall that in gambler's ruin, the expected number of steps is (N - i)/(p - q) when p ≠ q.\n\nBut in our case, p=0.6, q=0.4, so p - q = 0.2.\n\nTherefore, E(i) = (150 - i)/0.2.\n\nSo, for i=100, E(100) = (150 - 100)/0.2 = 50 / 0.2 = 250.\n\nSo, that matches the earlier result.\n\nBut let me verify this with another method. Let's consider the expected change in wealth per round.\n\nEach round, the expected change is 0.6*10 + 0.4*(-10) = 6 - 4 = 2 dollars.\n\nSo, the expected wealth after each round is increasing by 2 dollars.\n\nBut the target is 150, starting from 100, so we need to reach 50 more dollars.",
