    "chain_full": "Okay, so I have this problem about a casino game where I flip a biased coin. The coin lands heads 60% of the time and tails 40%. I start with $100, and each round I bet $10. If it's heads, I win $10, and if it's tails, I lose $10. The game ends when I either reach $150 (win) or go broke to $0 (loss). I need to find two things: the probability that I reach $150 before going broke, and the expected number of rounds until the game ends.\n\nHmm, this sounds like a classic gambler's ruin problem. I remember that in gambler's ruin, you have a gambler with finite wealth who bets on a game with probability p of winning and q=1-p of losing. The goal is to find the probability of reaching a certain wealth before going bankrupt.\n\nIn this case, the gambler starts with $100, and the target is $150. So, the total possible wealth is from $0 to $150. The probability of winning each bet is p=0.6, and losing is q=0.4.\n\nFirst, I need to recall the formula for the probability of reaching a certain wealth before ruin in a gambler's ruin problem. I think it's something like:\n\nIf the gambler starts with i dollars, the probability of reaching N before 0 is:\n\nP(i) = [1 - (q/p)^i] / [1 - (q/p)^N]\n\nBut wait, this formula is when p ≠ q. If p = q, then the probability is i/N. But in our case, p=0.6 and q=0.4, so p ≠ q.\n\nSo, plugging in the values, N is 150, and i is 100. So,\n\nP(100) = [1 - (0.4/0.6)^100] / [1 - (0.4/0.6)^150]\n\nLet me compute (0.4/0.6). That's 2/3, approximately 0.6667.\n\nSo, (2/3)^100 is a very small number, since 2/3 is less than 1, and raising it to the 100th power makes it extremely small. Similarly, (2/3)^150 is even smaller.\n\nTherefore, 1 - (2/3)^100 is approximately 1, and 1 - (2/3)^150 is also approximately 1. So, the probability P(100) is approximately 1 / 1 = 1. Wait, that can't be right. If p > q, the probability of reaching N before ruin should be higher than 0.5, right?\n\nWait, maybe I got the formula wrong. Let me double-check