        "text": "However, I'm not entirely sure if this combination is the best."
      },
      {
        "label": "backtracking",
        "text": "Maybe focusing on one intervention at a time could be more effective."
      },
      {
        "label": "example-testing",
        "text": "For example, if more lanes are added, it might lead to more accidents, which then leads to more repairs, which then leads to more funding for more lanes, creating a cycle."
      },
      {
        "label": "deduction",
        "text": "Alternatively, implementing congestion pricing could reduce peak traffic but might cause a shift towards public transport, which could be a double-edged sword."
      },
      {
        "label": "deduction",
        "text": "I also need to consider the equity implications."
      },
      {
        "label": "deduction",
        "text": "If congestion pricing leads to a shift towards public transport, it might reduce the need for private vehicles, which could be seen as a positive for equity."
      },
      {
        "label": "deduction",
        "text": "However, if it leads to a decrease in private vehicles, it might reduce the city's equity because more people rely on public transport instead."
      },
      {
        "label": "deduction",
        "text": "In conclusion, while each intervention has its own set of unintended consequences, the combination of all three might be the most sustainable because it addresses both immediate issues and unintended shifts."
      },
      {
        "label": "uncertainty-estimation",
        "text": "However, I'm still a bit uncertain."
      },
      {
        "label": "uncertainty-estimation",
        "text": "Maybe focusing on one intervention is better, but I think the combination is more comprehensive."
