        "text": "For example, if urgent care is overwhelmed (factor 1), it might lead to longer wait times (factor 2), which could cause patients to go elsewhere, slowing down processing times (factor 3)."
      },
      {
        "label": "deduction",
        "text": "This could create a cycle where urgent care is overwhelmed, leading to longer wait times, which then cause patients to go elsewhere, leading to slower processing times, which then cause patients to go to urgent care again, leading to a cycle."
      },
      {
        "label": "deduction",
        "text": "Alternatively, if we address factor 4 by encouraging patients to come to urgent care, it could reduce the number of patients treated elsewhere, thus reducing factor 2."
      },
      {
        "label": "deduction",
        "text": "This would allow other units to handle more patients, potentially increasing bed demand, which could lead to longer wait times (factor 2), which then could cause patients to go elsewhere, slowing down processing times (factor 3), and so on."
      },
      {
        "label": "deduction",
        "text": "In summary, each factor is interconnected, and addressing one can have a ripple effect on others."
      },
      {
        "label": "deduction",
        "text": "The key leverage points are urgent care, ambulance diversions, staff burnout, discharge processes, and reputation."
      }
    ],
